<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Cambridge,_Massachusetts> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Cambridge** ( [*KAYM-brij*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29) is a city in [Middlesex County, Massachusetts](:Middlesex_County%2C_Massachusetts), United States. Part of the [Boston metropolitan area](:Greater_Boston), at the [2020 U.S. Census](:2020_United_States_Census) the city's population was 118,403, making it the fourth most populous city in the state, behind [Boston](:Boston%2C_Massachusetts), [Worcester](:Worcester%2C_Massachusetts), and [Springfield](:Springfield%2C_Massachusetts). It is one of two [de jure](:De_jure) [county seats](:County_seat) of Middlesex County, although the county's executive government was abolished in 1997. Situated directly north of [Boston](:Boston), across the [Charles River](:Charles_River), it was named in honor of the [University of Cambridge](:University_of_Cambridge) in England, once also an important center of the [Puritan](:Puritan) theology embraced by the town's founders.[Harvard University](:Harvard_University), the [Massachusetts Institute of Technology](:Massachusetts_Institute_of_Technology) (MIT), [Lesley University](:Lesley_University), and [Hult International Business School](:Hult_International_Business_School) are in Cambridge, as was [Radcliffe College](:Radcliffe_College) before it merged with Harvard. [Kendall Square](:Kendall_Square) in Cambridge has been called \"the most innovative square mile on the planet\" owing to the high concentration of successful [startups](:Startup_company) that have emerged in the vicinity of the square since 2010."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
