<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Los_Angeles> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "[![](https://maps.wikimedia.org/img/osm-intl,8,34.05,-118.25,290x270.png?lang=en&domain=en.wikipedia.org&title=Los_Angeles&revid=1124735028&groups=_c23e5a1d0787a906b484ecfa87ebedfc0be5c7e9)](/wiki/Special:Map/8/34.05/-118.25/en)**Los Angeles** (  [*lawss AN-jəl-əs*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key); Template:Lang-es Template:IPA-es, lit. 'The Angels'), often referred to by its initials **L.A.**, is the [largest city](:List_of_municipalities_in_California) in the [state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [California](:California) and the [second most populous](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population) city in the [United States](:United_States) after [New York City](:New_York_City), as well as one of the world's most populous [megacities](:Megacities). Los Angeles is the commercial, financial, and cultural center of [Southern California](:Southern_California). With a population of roughly 3.9 million as of 2020[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Los_Angeles&action=edit), Los Angeles is known for its [Mediterranean climate](:Mediterranean_climate), ethnic and cultural diversity, [Hollywood film industry](:Cinema_of_the_United_States), and [sprawling metropolitan area](:Greater_Los_Angeles). The city of Los Angeles lies in [a basin](:Los_Angeles_Basin) in Southern California adjacent to the [Pacific Ocean](:Pacific_Ocean) extending through the [Santa Monica Mountains](:Santa_Monica_Mountains) and into the [San Fernando Valley](:San_Fernando_Valley). It covers about 469 square miles (1,210 km2), and is the [seat](:County_seat) of [Los Angeles County](:Los_Angeles_County%2C_California), which is the [most populous county in the United States](:List_of_the_most_populous_counties_in_the_United_States) with an estimated 9.86 million as of 2022[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Los_Angeles&action=edit).Home to the [Chumash](:Chumash_people) and [Tongva](:Tongva) [indigenous peoples](:Indigenous_people), the area that became Los Angeles was claimed by [Juan Rodríguez Cabrillo](:Juan_Rodr%C3%ADguez_Cabrillo) for [Spain](:Spanish_Empire) in 1542. The city was founded on September 4, 1781, under Spanish governor [Felipe de Neve](:Felipe_de_Neve), on the village of [Yaanga](:Yaanga). It became a part of [Mexico](:First_Mexican_Empire) in 1821 following the [Mexican War of Independence](:Mexican_War_of_Independence). In 1848, at the end of the [Mexican–American War](:Mexican%E2%80%93American_War), Los Angeles and the rest of California were purchased as part of the [Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo](:Treaty_of_Guadalupe_Hidalgo), and thus became part of the United States. Los Angeles was [incorporated](:Municipal_corporation) as a municipality on April 4, 1850, five months before California achieved [statehood](:U%2ES%2E_state). The discovery of [oil](:Petroleum) in the 1890s brought rapid growth to the city. The city was further expanded with the completion of the [Los Angeles Aqueduct](:Los_Angeles_Aqueduct) in 1913, which delivers water from [Eastern California](:Eastern_California).Los Angeles has a diverse economy, and hosts businesses in a broad range of professional and cultural fields. It also has the [busiest container port](:Port_of_Los_Angeles) in the Americas. In 2018, the Los Angeles metropolitan area had a [gross metropolitan product](:Gross_metropolitan_product) of over $1.0 trillion, making it the city with the [third-largest GDP](:List_of_cities_by_GDP) in the world, after [New York City](:New_York_metropolitan_area) and [Tokyo](:Tokyo_metropolitan_area). Los Angeles hosted the [1932](:1932_Summer_Olympics) and [1984 Summer Olympics](:1984_Summer_Olympics) and will host the [2028 Summer Olympics](:2028_Summer_Olympics). More recently, statewide [droughts in California](:Droughts_in_California) have strained both the city’s and Los Angeles County’s [water security](:Water_security)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
