<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Verizon_(mobile_network)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Verizon** is an American [wireless network operator](:Cellular_network) that previously operated as a separate division of [Verizon Communications](:Verizon_Communications) under the name **Verizon Wireless**. In a 2019 reorganization, Verizon moved the wireless products and services into the divisions Verizon Consumer and [Verizon Business](:Verizon_Business), and stopped using the Verizon Wireless name. Verizon is the [largest wireless carrier in the United States](:List_of_United_States_wireless_communications_service_providers), with 142.8 million subscribers at the end of Q4 2021.The company is headquartered in [Basking Ridge, New Jersey](:Basking_Ridge%2C_New_Jersey). It was founded in 2000 as a [joint venture](:Joint_venture) of American telecommunications firm [Bell Atlantic](:Bell_Atlantic), which would soon become [Verizon Communications](:Verizon_Communications), and [British](:United_Kingdom) [multinational](:Multinational_corporation) telecommunications company [Vodafone](:Vodafone). Verizon Communications became the sole owner in 2014 after buying Vodafone's 45-percent stake in the company.It operates a national [4G](:4G) [LTE](:LTE_%28telecommunication%29) network covering about 99 percent of the U.S. population, which in the second half of 2020 won or tied for top honors in each category of the [RootMetrics](:RootMetrics) RootScore Reports. Verizon Wireless offers [mobile phone](:Mobile_phone) services through a variety of devices. Its LTE in Rural America Program, with 21 rural wireless carriers participating, covers 2.7 million potential users in 169 rural counties. Verizon Wireless announced in 2015 that it was developing a [5G](:5G), or fifth-generation, network."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
