<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dayton,_Ohio> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Dayton** ( ) is the [sixth-largest](:List_of_cities_in_Ohio) city in the [state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Ohio](:Ohio) and the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Montgomery County](:Montgomery_County%2C_Ohio). A small part of the city extends into [Greene County](:Greene_County%2C_Ohio). The 2020 [U.S. census estimate](:United_States_Census) put the city population at 137,644, while [Greater Dayton](:Dayton_metropolitan_area) was estimated to be at 814,049 residents. The Combined Statistical Area (CSA) was 1,086,512. This makes Dayton the fourth-largest metropolitan area in Ohio and 73rd in the United States. Dayton is within Ohio's [Miami Valley](:Miami_Valley) region, 50 miles (80 km) north of the [Greater Cincinnati](:Cincinnati%E2%80%93Northern_Kentucky_metropolitan_area) area.Ohio's borders are within 500 miles (800 km) of roughly 60 percent of the country's population and manufacturing infrastructure, making the Dayton area a logistical [centroid](:Supply_chain_management%23Supply_chain) for manufacturers, suppliers, and shippers. Dayton also hosts significant [research and development](:Research_and_development) in fields like industrial, [aeronautical](:Aeronautics), and [astronautical](:Astronautics) engineering that have led to many technological innovations. Much of this innovation is due in part to [Wright-Patterson Air Force Base](:Wright-Patterson_Air_Force_Base) and its place in the community. With the decline of heavy manufacturing, Dayton's businesses have diversified into a [service economy](:Service_economy) that includes insurance and legal sectors as well as healthcare and government sectors.Along with [defense](:United_States_Department_of_Defense) and [aerospace](:Aerospace), [healthcare](:Healthcare) accounts for much of the Dayton area's economy. Hospitals in the Greater Dayton area have an estimated combined employment of nearly 32,000 and a yearly economic impact of $6.8 billion. It is estimated that [Premier Health Partners](:Premier_Health_Partners), a hospital network, contributes more than $2 billion a year to the region through operating, employment, and capital expenditures. In 2011, Dayton was rated the #3 city in the nation by [HealthGrades](:HealthGrades) for excellence in healthcare.Dayton is also noted for its association with aviation; the city is the birthplace of [Orville Wright](:Orville_Wright). Other well-known individuals born in the city include poet [Paul Laurence Dunbar](:Paul_Laurence_Dunbar) and entrepreneur [John H. Patterson](:John_Henry_Patterson_%28NCR_owner%29). Dayton is also known for its many [patents](:Patent), [inventions](:Invention), and [inventors](:Inventor), most notably the [Wright brothers](:Wright_brothers)' invention of [powered flight](:Powered_flight). In 2007 Dayton was a part of the top 100 cities in America. In 2008, 2009, and 2010, [*Site Selection* magazine](:Site_Selection_%28magazine%29) ranked Dayton the #1 mid-sized metropolitan area in the nation for economic development. Also in 2010, Dayton was named one of the best places in the United States for college graduates to find a job."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
