<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Shenzhen> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Shenzhen** (; ; [Chinese](:Simplified_Chinese_characters): [深圳](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/深圳); [pinyin](:Pinyin): *[Shēnzhèn](https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Shēnzhèn)*; Mandarin pronunciation:  ), also historically known as **Sham Chun**, is a major [sub-provincial](:Sub-provincial_division) city and one of the [special economic zones](:Special_economic_zones_of_China) of China. The city is located on the east bank of the [Pearl River](:Pearl_River_%28China%29) [estuary](:Estuary) on the central coast of southern province of [Guangdong](:Guangdong), bordering [Hong Kong](:Hong_Kong) to the south, [Dongguan](:Dongguan) to the north, and [Huizhou](:Huizhou) to the northeast. With a population of 17.56 million as of 2020, Shenzhen is the third most populous city by urban population in China after [Shanghai](:Shanghai) and [Beijing](:Beijing). Shenzhen is a global center in [technology](:List_of_technology_centers), [research](:List_of_cities_by_scientific_output), [manufacturing](:Economy_of_China%23Industry_and_manufacturing), [business and economics](:Shenzhen%23Economy), [finance](:Global_Financial_Centres_Index), [tourism](:Shenzhen%23Tourism) and [transportation](:Transport_in_China), and the [Port of Shenzhen](:Port_of_Shenzhen) is the [world's fourth busiest container port](:List_of_busiest_container_ports).Shenzhen roughly follows the administrative boundaries of [Bao'an County](:Bao%27an_County), which was established since imperial times. The southern portion of Bao'an County was seized by the [British](:British_Empire) after the [Opium Wars](:Opium_Wars) and became Hong Kong, while the village of Shenzhen was situated on the border. Due to the completion of a [train station](:Shenzhen_railway_station) that was the last stop on the Mainland Chinese section of the [railway between Guangzhou and Kowloon](:Kowloon%E2%80%93Canton_Railway), Shenzhen's economy grew and became a market town and later a city by 1979, absorbing Bao'an County for the next decade.In the early 1980s, [economic reforms](:Chinese_economic_reform) introduced by [Deng Xiaoping](:Deng_Xiaoping) resulted in the city becoming the first special economic zone of China due to its close proximity to Hong Kong, attracting [foreign direct investment](:Foreign_direct_investment) and migrants searching for opportunities. In thirty years, the city's economy and population boomed and has since emerged as a hub for technology, international trade, and finance. It is the home to the [Shenzhen Stock Exchange](:Shenzhen_Stock_Exchange), one of the [largest stock exchanges in the world](:List_of_stock_exchanges) by market capitalization and the [Guangdong Free-Trade Zone](:Guangdong_Free-Trade_Zone). Shenzhen is ranked as an Alpha- ([global first-tier](:Global_city%29) city by the [Globalization and World Cities Research Network](:Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network). Its nominal GDP has surpassed neighboring cities of [Guangzhou](:Guangzhou) and Hong Kong and is now among the top ten cities with the largest economies in the world. Shenzhen also has the [eighth](:Global_Financial_Centres_Index) most competitive and largest financial center in the world, the eighth-most [Fortune Global 500](:Fortune_Global_500) headquarters of any city in the world, [fifth-highest number of billionaires](:List_of_cities_by_number_of_billionaires) of any city in the world, [the second largest number of skyscrapers](:List_of_cities_with_the_most_skyscrapers) of any city in the world, the [28th largest scientific research output](:List_of_cities_by_scientific_output) of any city in the world, and [several notable educational institutions](:List_of_universities_and_colleges_in_Guangdong), such as [Shenzhen University](:Shenzhen_University), [Southern University of Science and Technology](:Southern_University_of_Science_and_Technology), and [Shenzhen Technology University](:Shenzhen_Technology_University).Due to the city being a leading global technology hub, Shenzhen has been dubbed by media *China's [Silicon Valley](:Silicon_Valley)*. The city's entrepreneurial, innovative, and competitive-based culture has resulted in the city being home to numerous small-time manufacturers or software companies. Several of these firms became large technology corporations such as phone manufacturer [Huawei](:Huawei), holding company [Tencent](:Tencent), and drone-maker [DJI](:DJI). As an important international city, Shenzhen hosts numerous national and international events every year, such as the [2011 Summer Universiade](:2011_Summer_Universiade) and the [China Hi-Tech Fair](:China_Hi-Tech_Fair). Shenzhen's rapid success has resulted in the Chinese government turning Shenzhen into a model city for other cities in China to follow."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
