<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Vancouver> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Vancouver** (  [*van-KOO-vər*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29) is a major city in [western Canada](:Western_Canada), located in the [Lower Mainland](:Lower_Mainland) region of [British Columbia](:British_Columbia). As the [most populous city](:List_of_cities_in_British_Columbia) in the province, the [2021 census](:2021_Canadian_census) recorded 662,248 people in the city, up from 631,486 in 2016. The [Greater Vancouver area](:Greater_Vancouver) had a population of 2.6 million in 2021, making it the [third-largest metropolitan area in Canada](:List_of_census_metropolitan_areas_and_agglomerations_in_Canada%23List). Greater Vancouver, along with the [Fraser Valley](:Fraser_Valley_Regional_District), comprises the [Lower Mainland](:Lower_Mainland) with a regional population of over 3 million. Vancouver has the highest population density in Canada, with over 5,700 people per square kilometre, and fourth highest in North America (after New York City, San Francisco, and Mexico City).Vancouver is one of the most [ethnically](:Ethnic_origins_of_people_in_Canada) and [linguistically](:Languages_of_Canada) diverse cities in Canada: 49.3 percent of its residents are not native English speakers, 47.8 percent are native speakers of neither English nor French, and 54.5 percent of residents belong to [visible minority](:Visible_minority) groups. It has been consistently ranked one of the [most livable cities](:Most_livable_cities) in Canada and in the world. In terms of [housing affordability](:Affordable_housing), Vancouver is also one of [the most expensive cities in Canada](:Affordable_housing_in_Canada) and [in the world](:Affordable_housing_by_country). Vancouver [plans to become the greenest city](:Vancouver%27s_greenest_city_action_plan) in the world. [Vancouverism](:Vancouverism) is the city's [urban planning](:Urban_planning) design philosophy.Indigenous settlement of Vancouver began more than 10,000 years ago, and included the [Squamish](:Squamish_people), [Musqueam](:Musqueam_Indian_Band), and [Tsleil-Waututh (Burrard)](:Tsleil-Waututh_First_Nation) peoples. The beginnings of the modern city, which was originally named [Gastown](:Gastown), grew around the site of a makeshift tavern on the western edges of [Hastings Mill](:Hastings_Mill) that was built on July 1, 1867, and owned by proprietor [Gassy Jack](:John_Deighton). The original site is marked by the Gastown steam clock. Gastown then formally registered as a [townsite](:Townsite) dubbed [Granville](:Granville%2C_British_Columbia), [Burrard Inlet](:Burrard_Inlet). The city was renamed \"Vancouver\" in 1886, through a deal with the [Canadian Pacific Railway](:Canadian_Pacific_Railway) (CPR). The Canadian Pacific transcontinental railway was extended to the city by 1887. The city's large natural seaport on the [Pacific Ocean](:Pacific_Ocean) became a vital link in the trade between [Asia-Pacific](:Asia-Pacific), [East Asia](:East_Asia), [Europe](:Europe), and [Eastern Canada](:Eastern_Canada).Vancouver has hosted many international conferences and events, including the [1954 Commonwealth Games](:1954_British_Empire_and_Commonwealth_Games), [UN Habitat I](:Habitat_I), [Expo 86](:Expo_86), [APEC Canada 1997](:APEC_Canada_1997), the [World Police and Fire Games](:World_Police_and_Fire_Games) in 1989 and 2009; several matches of [2015 FIFA Women's World Cup](:2015_FIFA_Women%27s_World_Cup) including the finals at [BC Place](:BC_Place) in [Downtown Vancouver](:Downtown_Vancouver), and the [2010 Winter Olympics](:2010_Winter_Olympics) and [Paralympics](:2010_Winter_Paralympics) which were held in Vancouver and [Whistler](:Whistler%2C_British_Columbia), a resort community 125 km (78 mi) north of the city. In 1969, [Greenpeace](:Greenpeace) was founded in Vancouver. The city became the permanent home to [TED conferences](:TED_%28conference%29) in 2014.As of 2016[[update]](//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Vancouver&action=edit), the [Port of Vancouver](:Port_of_Vancouver) is the fourth-largest port by tonnage in the Americas, the busiest and largest in Canada, and the most diversified port in North America. While forestry remains its largest industry, Vancouver is well known as an urban centre surrounded by nature, making [tourism](:Tourism_in_Canada) its second-largest industry. Major film production studios in Vancouver and nearby [Burnaby](:Burnaby) have turned Greater Vancouver and nearby areas into one of the largest [film production](:Principal_photography) centres in North America, earning it the nickname \"[Hollywood North](:Hollywood_North)\"."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/All-Steel_Equipment_Company> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Allsteel was founded in 1912 as Allsteelequip Company and started out manufacturing custom metal objects, especially electrical equipment. The name was changed in 1929 to All-Steel-Equip Company. In the 1920s, the company began to branch out and started producing kitchen cabinets and refrigerated food lockers. Requests for metal file cabinets were becoming common, so the company bought out a supplier, the Aurora Metal Cabinet Company, in 1936 to enter this market."@en .
