<http://dbpedia.org/resource/San_Jose,_California> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**San Jose**, officially **San José** (; Template:IPA-es; Error: {{language with name/for}}: missing language tag or language name ([help](:Template%3ALanguage_with_name/for%29)), is a major city in the U.S. state of [California](:California) that is the cultural, financial, and political center of [Silicon Valley](:Silicon_Valley) and largest city in [Northern California](:Northern_California) by both population and area. With a 2020 population of 1,013,240, it is the most populous city in both the [Bay Area](:Bay_Area) and the [San Jose-San Francisco-Oakland Combined Statistical Area](:San_Jose%E2%80%93San_Francisco%E2%80%93Oakland%2C_CA_Combined_Statistical_Area), which contain 7.7 million and 9.7 million people respectively, the [third-most populous city in California](:List_of_largest_California_cities_by_population) (after [Los Angeles](:Los_Angeles) and [San Diego](:San_Diego) and ahead of [San Francisco](:San_Francisco%29), and the [tenth-most populous in the United States](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population). Located in the center of the [Santa Clara Valley](:Santa_Clara_Valley) on the southern shore of [San Francisco Bay](:San_Francisco_Bay), San Jose covers an area of 179.97 sq mi (466.1 km2). San Jose is the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Santa Clara County](:Santa_Clara_County%2C_California) and the main component of the San Jose–Sunnyvale–Santa Clara Metropolitan Statistical Area, with an estimated population of around two million residents in 2018.San Jose is notable for its [innovation](:Innovation), cultural diversity, [affluence](:Affluence), and sunny and mild [Mediterranean climate](:Mediterranean_climate). Its connection to the booming [high tech](:High_tech) industry phenomenon known as [Silicon Valley](:Silicon_Valley) sparked Mayor [Tom McEnery](:Tom_McEnery) to adopt the city the motto of \"Capital of Silicon Valley\" in 1988. Major global tech companies including [Cisco Systems](:Cisco_Systems), [eBay](:EBay), [Adobe Inc.](:Adobe_Inc%2E), [PayPal](:PayPal), [Broadcom](:Broadcom_Inc%2E), [Samsung](:Samsung), [Acer](:Acer_Inc%2E), and [Zoom](:Zoom_Video_Communications) maintain their headquarters in San Jose. San Jose is one of the wealthiest major cities in the world, with the third-highest GDP per capita (after [Zürich](:Z%C3%BCrich%2C_Switzerland) and [Oslo](:Oslo%2C_Norway%29) and the fifth-most expensive housing market. It is home to the world's largest [overseas Vietnamese](:Overseas_Vietnamese) population, a Hispanic community that makes up over 40% of the city's residents, and historic [Japanese](:Japantown_%28San_Jose%29) and [Portuguese](:Little_Portugal%2C_San_Jose) neighborhoods.Before the [arrival of the Spanish](:Spanish_colonization_of_the_Americas), the area around San Jose was inhabited by the [Tamien nation](:Tamyen_people) of the [Ohlone](:Ohlone) [peoples of California](:Indigenous_peoples_of_California). San Jose was founded on November 29, 1777, as the *[Pueblo](:Pueblo) de San José de [Guadalupe](:Our_Lady_of_Guadalupe)*, the first city founded in [the Californias](:The_Californias). It then became a part of [Mexico](:Mexico) in 1821 after the [Mexican War of Independence](:Mexican_War_of_Independence). Following the American [Conquest of California](:Conquest_of_California) during the [Mexican–American War](:Mexican%E2%80%93American_War), the territory [was ceded](:Mexican_Cession) to the United States in 1848. After California achieved statehood two years later, San Jose became the state's first capital. Following [World War II](:World_War_II), San Jose experienced an economic boom, with a rapid population growth and aggressive [annexation](:Municipal_annexation) of nearby cities and communities carried out in the 1950s and 1960s. The rapid growth of the high-technology and electronicsindustries further accelerated the transition from an agricultural center to an urbanized metropolitan area. Results of the [1990 U.S. Census](:1990_United_States_Census) indicated that San Jose had officially surpassed [San Francisco](:San_Francisco) as the most populous city in Northern California. By the 1990s, San Jose had become the global center for the high tech and internet industries, making it California's fastest-growing economy."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
